palace  in jeddah saudi arabia  a chandelier like this can cost up to  000 euros  we work for a very small niche which we  discovered 10 years ago  it's a design-oriented niche that  appreciates beauty  but the work the quality and the  attention to detail that go into our  products has its price  in berlin they created a lighting  concept for a luxury hotel suite  their chandeliers also grace the homes  of the rich and famous exclusive clients  looking for an exclusive adornment  it is a favish cut meter via  some of our designs are very colorful  and that's what makes us special  we're rather daring in our use of color  at first people found that shocking when  they saw it  but now 10 years on people have gotten  used to it  their office and showroom is located in  the heart of munich  the two designers draw inspiration from  city life and their urban surroundings  even though most of their clients come  from abroad they've also sold a few  items here in munich  like this chandelier which adorns the  showroom of a local upscale boutique  when you turn on the light it's just fun  we've had clients say that they spend an  hour at the dinner table because the  light's so lovely  by this  stream  traditional crystal chandeliers are  often heavy and look and feel  but in the hands of windfall's designers  they're transformed into one-of-a-kind  creations  delicate airy and contemporary  well it's time for a bit of sightseeing  euromag set off on for ulu in northern  finland it's a great spot to explore and  it attracts visitors for its beautiful  and peaceful countryside you can also  get your fill of hiking paddling and  mountain biking not to mention some  finnish tango  the region around olu is known for its  beautiful forests and lakes  the city itself is situated at the mouth  of the olyoke river  200 years ago uru was a major trading  center of woodtar to the rest of  northern europe  today the region draws visitors in  search of tranquility and beautiful  scenery  i come from mexico the mexico city so  there is like  absolutely the opposite right here we  were  walking around and sometimes there is no  one no human to be seen and it's  beautiful  from olo a ferry brings visitors to  hailuto still something of an insider's  tip  about a thousand people live on the  small island surrounded by stunning  scenery  among them ira's palkanon and her family  she also offers tours for visitors  people often ask me why is it so quiet  here did something happen  as soon as you get away from the only  road on the island it's absolutely quiet  all you hear is the wind and the birds  tourists can enjoy all the lovely  landscapes on view here  they can also digest nature's bounty  mushrooms and berries here belong to  everyone  this  we've already gathered 120 kilos of them  this season  reindeer also love the mushrooms  the herds belong to local farmers but  they're allowed to roam free  the animals are shy but they don't seem  to be afraid of cars  juhani vikkowin's family has bred  reindeer for four generations  he has a farm in taiwan about 150  kilometers from olu where he also offers  guided tours for visitors  usually they encounter a reindeer or two  but  usually there's a large herd grazing  here  but i saw a bear in the forest this  morning  so the reindeer are probably afraid and  hiding somewhere  reindeer meat plays an important role in  finnish cuisine  at the izociote hotel a two-hour drive  west of olu it's served in a traditional  way with lingonberries  and while you're digging in you can  feast your eyes on mount izociote in the  distance  the surrounding national park is a  popular place for hiking in the summer  and autumn  but winter is the high season here from  november to may there's snow everywhere  and lots of it there are all sorts of  ways to enjoy it from snowy hikes to  reindeer safaris  you're guaranteed snow here even if an  ulu 100 kilometers away there isn't any  the tourists come here to experience the  deep snow and the northern lights  the finns love nature so much that they  also socialize outdoors  their traditional dance pavilions are  usually situated by the shores of a lake  or river surrounded by trees  many host ballroom dancing once or twice  a month you can try your foot or  everything from tango to waltz to  traditional finnish dances  and local customs are important here too  the men ask the women to dance  two songs together and then the man  leads the woman back to the side  you don't have to talk when you're  dancing if you don't want to  hurry  up  it depends on whether you come alone or  with  so from the sound of it the locals in  finland know that silence is golden  and that includes out on the dance floor  that's all we have time for today you  can visit us on our facebook page and  let us know your thoughts that's it for  me and and the rest of your remarks crew  for now thanks for watching and we'll  see you tomorrow bye  so  you
 

